7. Diaphragm."},{"label":"C","letter":"C","text":"Describe the remarkable fact mentioned in the stem.","marks":3,"answer":"Oxygen absorption in the human body takes place through the lungs. Due to the pressure difference between the alveoli and blood, oxygen enters the blood by diffusion. After oxygen enters the arterial blood from the lungs, it is transported in two ways: a small amount of oxygen is dissolved in the blood plasma, while most oxygen forms a temporary compound called oxyhemoglobin by loosely binding with the iron part of hemoglobin. Oxygen can easily detach from oxyhemoglobin.
\(\text{Hemoglobin} + \text{Oxygen} \rightarrow \text{Oxyhemoglobin (Temporary Compound)}\)
\(\text{Oxyhemoglobin} \rightarrow \text{Free Oxygen} + \text{Hemoglobin}\)
After reaching the capillaries, oxygen separates and first penetrates the membrane of the red blood cell, then passes through the capillary wall to enter the lymph. Finally it passes from lymph to the cell membrane to reach the cell."},{"label":"D","letter":"D","text":"Analyze the diseases mentioned in the stem.","marks":4,"answer":"The diseases mentioned in the stimulus are lung cancer and pneumonia.
Among all types of cancer, lung cancer has the highest prevalence. In our country, lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer-related deaths among men. Tuberculosis or any form of pneumonia can cause lesions in the lungs, which may later transform into cancer. One of the primary causes of lung cancer is smoking. Lung cancer can also develop due to exposure to air and environmental pollution, or to pollutants in one's residence or workplace.
The symptoms of lung cancer include:
- Persistent dry cough and chest pain;
- Hoarseness, weight loss, and loss of appetite;
- Asthma, frequent fevers;
- Repeated infections of bronchitis or pneumonia.
If these symptoms appear, it is essential to consult a doctor immediately. After diagnosis, treatment should follow the doctor's advice. If necessary, radiation therapy may be administered, where cancer cells are destroyed using radioactive radiation.
Pneumonia is a disease of the lungs. It is caused by the bacteria Streptococcus pneumoniae. Excessive exposure to cold can lead to this disease. It may also occur after measles or bronchitis due to catching a cold. The symptoms of this disease include:
- Accumulation of mucus-like fluid in the lungs that causes phlegm;
- Cough and difficulty in breathing;
- Increased body temperature, meaning high fever;
- In advanced stages, rattling sounds in the chest and severe breathing difficulties.
Pneumonia patients must receive proper treatment as per the doctor's advice. Additionally, they should be given warm, nutritious liquid food and plenty of water to drink.
